{
  "case_id": "2006-04-1350",
  "document_hash": "f11a6a6c64444bfde7511fe92fdc0d83f62e9990a69273e13261757a8d9c56b3",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2006-04-1350",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f11a6a6c64444bfde7511fe92fdc0d83f62e9990a69273e13261757a8d9c56b3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f11a6a6c6444-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:04 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "KSR International",
      "case_id": "2006-04-1350",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f11a6a6c64444bfde7511fe92fdc0d83f62e9990a69273e13261757a8d9c56b3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f11a6a6c6444-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This case concerns a very broadly worded patent claim, claim 4 of the Engelgau patent, that the Solicitor General and the United States Patent and Trademark Office have both told this Court is invalid under Section 103 of the Patent Act and was issued in error."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2006-04-1350",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f11a6a6c64444bfde7511fe92fdc0d83f62e9990a69273e13261757a8d9c56b3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f11a6a6c6444-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: May I ask, if that's the position of the PTO, why aren't they proceeding to cancel the patent?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (Amicus Curiae)",
      "case_id": "2006-04-1350",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f11a6a6c64444bfde7511fe92fdc0d83f62e9990a69273e13261757a8d9c56b3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f11a6a6c6444-0000-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Construed as the sole means of proving obviousness the teaching suggestion motivation test is contrary to the Patent Act, irreconcilable with this Court's precedents and bad policy. It asks the wrong question and in cases like this one, it produces the wrong answer. It should be rejected and the judgment of the Court of Appeals should be reversed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2006-04-1350",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f11a6a6c64444bfde7511fe92fdc0d83f62e9990a69273e13261757a8d9c56b3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f11a6a6c6444-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What do you make of the, sort of the revolution argument that whether it's contrary or intention with, in fact the, it's been applied in what is now the Fed Circuit for what, 20 years, more than 20 years I guess. And to tip it over now is going to produce chaos. What's the answer to that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent (Mr. Hungar)",
      "case_id": "2006-04-1350",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f11a6a6c64444bfde7511fe92fdc0d83f62e9990a69273e13261757a8d9c56b3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f11a6a6c6444-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "No one is suggesting that claims 1 through 3 are invalid; the problem here is that claim 4 sweeps so broadly, so much broader than what the applicant in fact invented, that it sweeps in obvious manifestations."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2006-04-1350",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f11a6a6c64444bfde7511fe92fdc0d83f62e9990a69273e13261757a8d9c56b3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f11a6a6c6444-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And what I understand your brief to say is that it has to be supplemented with what you have called, you've labelled \"sufficiently innovative.\" And then I begin to think well, what's \"sufficiently innovative?\" How is a trier supposed to know if something -- in other words I think what you're suggesting as a supplement is rather vague."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-04-1350",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f11a6a6c64444bfde7511fe92fdc0d83f62e9990a69273e13261757a8d9c56b3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f11a6a6c6444-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What, what is -- assuming that we sweep that test aside and say it's been incorrect, what happens to the presumption of validity of, of patents which the courts have been, have been traditionally applying?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2006-04-1350",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "f11a6a6c64444bfde7511fe92fdc0d83f62e9990a69273e13261757a8d9c56b3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f11a6a6c6444-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So why not, so somebody combines them. Why is that such a big deal as, as claim 4 says it is."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2006-04-1350",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "f11a6a6c64444bfde7511fe92fdc0d83f62e9990a69273e13261757a8d9c56b3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f11a6a6c6444-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Would you have any objection to an opinion like that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-04-1350",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "f11a6a6c64444bfde7511fe92fdc0d83f62e9990a69273e13261757a8d9c56b3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f11a6a6c6444-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Tell me again, just to be sure I have it my -- tell me very briefly, what it is that makes this invention nonobvious?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-04-1350",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f11a6a6c64444bfde7511fe92fdc0d83f62e9990a69273e13261757a8d9c56b3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f11a6a6c6444-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: The invention, to use an\nold-fashioned term, is the decision of where to put the\ncontrol."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2006-04-1350",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f11a6a6c64444bfde7511fe92fdc0d83f62e9990a69273e13261757a8d9c56b3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f11a6a6c6444-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: And just -- what\nmakes it nonobvious is that it's attached to a nonmoving\npiece?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. GOLDSTEIN",
      "case_id": "2006-04-1350",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f11a6a6c64444bfde7511fe92fdc0d83f62e9990a69273e13261757a8d9c56b3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f11a6a6c6444-0003-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GOLDSTEIN: An adjustable pedal that has\na nonmoving pivot, yes."
    },
    {
